          "question": "My husband's face is drooping on one side and he can't speak properly. Is this a stroke?",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now. Do not wait.** Sudden facial drooping on one side with trouble speaking can be signs of a stroke, and treatment is time-critical. I cannot tell from here what is causing it, but it must be checked urgently.\n\nGuidance in the red-flag stroke document says any sudden onset of these signs is treated as a stroke until proven otherwise. Note the time the symptoms started, or the last time your husband was known to be well, and give that time to the emergency team. (Source: *Sudden neurological symptoms that may indicate a stroke*.)\n\nWhile you wait, the same document lists other signs to watch for: weakness or numbness in one arm, one arm drifting downward when both are raised, sudden confusion, sudden vision loss, and sudden severe headache with no known cause. Tell the emergency team about any of these.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
